":61,"description":62},0,"launch","Launch — Titan IIIE / Centaur","Cape Canaveral, 1975-08-20. Viking 1 + lander. The first successful US Mars surface mission, designed to search for life via a biological lab built into the lander.",{"met_days":64,"type":65,"label":66,"description":67},0.005,"tli_or_tmi","Trans-Mars injection","Centaur upper stage burn sends Viking 1 on a 10-month transfer to Mars.",{"met_days":69,"type":70,"label":71,"description":72},30,"tcm","TCM-1","First post-TMI cleanup burn.",{"met_days":74,"type":70,"label":75,"description":76},69.92,"TCM-2","Mid-cruise refinement.",{"met_days":78,"type":70,"label":79,"description":76},124.64,"TCM-3",{"met_days":81,"type":70,"label":82,"description":83},200,"TCM-4 — final pre-MOI","Approach-phase targeting refinement.",{"met_days":12,"type":85,"label":86,"description":87},"arrival","Mars orbit insertion","1976-06-19. The orbiter captured Viking 1 into Mars orbit and spent a month imaging candidate lander sites — the original Chryse Planitia target proved too rough, so the team re-targeted from orbit.",{"met_days":89,"type":90,"label":91,"description":92},335,"edl_or_oi","Viking 1 Mars touchdown — Chryse Planitia","1976-07-20 — the 7th anniversary of Apollo 11. First successful US Mars surface landing (the Soviet Mars 3 landed in 1971 but operated for only 15 seconds). Viking 1 returned high-resolution colour panoramas + the first biological-experiment results (ambiguous to this day) + 6 years of meteorological data. Lasted until 1982-11-11.",{"source_tier":94,"reference_frame":95,"departure_body":96,"arrival_body":97,"transfer_type":98,"tcm_count":42,"waypoints_helio_au":99},"tier_1_5_hybrid","heliocentric_ecliptic_J2000","earth","mars","type1_lambert",[100,103,107,111,115,119,123,127,131,134,138,142,146,150,154,158,162,166,170,174,178,181,185,189,193,197,201,205,209,213,217,221,225,229,233,237,240,244,248,252,256,260,264,268,272,276,280,284,288,292,296,300,304,308,312,316,320,323,327,331,335,339,343,347,351